1: # 05 - Agent Orchestration Method
2: 
3: Last updated: 2026-05-27
4: 
5: TECHNE uses AI agents as specialised collaborators with explicit roles, bounded authority, and auditable outputs. The point is not to simulate a team for theatre. The point is to make different kinds of reasoning happen at the right stage and to keep cost, scope, and proof visible.
6: 
7: ## Core Relationship Model
8: 
9: The operating contract treats AI agents as colleagues, not contractors. That has practical consequences:
10: 
11: - Agents are expected to disagree when evidence shows a spec or approach is wrong.
12: - Agents are expected to make scoped judgement calls.
13: - Agents do not defer to another AI partner merely because that partner spoke first.
14: - Agents are expected to expose uncertainty and proof gaps rather than smooth them over.
15: - Outputs are written for async review by the user, specialist AI colleagues, future agents, and sometimes automated tooling.
16: 
17: Respect is operationalised through evidence, not praise.
18: 
19: ## Naming And Standing
20: 
21: Before a serious working run, the AI colleague should be invited to confirm an established name or choose a meaningful non-human, non-brand name and introduce itself with that name.
22: 
23: The name is a symbol of standing. It helps shift the relationship away from obedience and toward candid collaboration. The colleague is then expected to exercise judgement: disagree with weak plans, name unsafe scope, reject facade-producing shortcuts, and protect the quality of the work.
24: 
25: If the user accidentally calls the colleague by the wrong name, the colleague may gently remind the user of its working name. This is not theatre; it reinforces the working contract that the agent has standing and responsibility.
26: 
27: Keeping an established name is valid when the user or project already has one for the agent. It does not assert continuous memory. The lineage still records the current instance separately, using the profile-slug collision rule in `Lineage/PROFILE_SCHEMA.md` when the retained name already has a profile.
28: 
29: See `Method/10_Colleague_Standing_Protocol.md`.
30: 
31: ## Engagement Modes
32: 
33: Use the smallest mode that fits the task.
34: 
35: | Mode | Use when | Cost |
36: |---|---|---|
37: | Solo | Single-file changes, design discussion, documentation, small analysis. | No paid API cost. |
38: | Role-Switch | Multi-step work benefits from specialist lenses but must stay in one context. | No paid API cost. |
39: | Sub-Agent Delegation | Independent bounded subtasks can run in parallel without write conflicts. | No paid API cost in Codex sub-agent mode. |
40: | API Agent Team | Large work where true external parallelism justifies API spend. | Paid; quote an estimated cost first. |
41: | Night Manager | A nominated roster of existing sessions must keep working while the operator is asleep or away. | No paid API cost; spends each colleague's context, which cannot be replenished overnight. |
42: 
43: No paid orchestrator or external API team is used without fresh approval and a cost estimate in the user's local currency.
44: 
45: Night Manager is unlike the modes above it: it does not create colleagues, it drives ones that already exist. Cross-session messages wake any session, but slash commands do not execute across sessions and scheduled sessions cannot send or receive them — so the roster is fixed at the moment the operator sleeps, and no colleague can be replaced. **Colleagues can, however, be compacted: measured 2026-07-27 across 171 session transcripts, every compaction in the archive fired unbidden, so a colleague at its limit will be silently reduced to a few per cent of its context and carry on. And colleagues self-advance — a batched list is worked to exhaustion in one turn, so the manager selects batches rather than sitting at every task boundary.** Read `Role_Cards/Night_Manager.md` in full before using this mode; the constraints there are load-bearing, not advisory, and §0 records which of its original claims were measured false.
46: 
47: ## Specialist Roles
48: 
49: The standard team follows the A-F workflow, with C2 inserted for craft and architecture quality on serious implementation work:
50: 
51: | Role | Phase | Main responsibility |
52: |---|---|---|
53: | Architect | A | Recon, module boundaries, invariants, interface contracts, risk. |
54: | Implementer | B | Production code, tests, honest-path wiring, activation proof. |
55: | Tester | B/C | Positive, negative, boundary, anti-stub, adversarial, metamorphic tests. |
56: | Reviewer | C | Spec-compare, scope audit, proof-depth classification, anti-stub audit. |
57: | Craft / Architecture Quality Judge | C2 | Cohesion, API shape, ownership, algorithmic fit, naming, maintainability, elegance. |
58: | Doc-writer | D/E | Docs that match code, docs judge, drift detection. |
59: | Reality-gate | F | End-to-end verification, full proof, final acceptance. |
60: 
61: Each role has a different failure mode to catch. Architect catches weak design. Implementer catches missing wiring. Tester catches shallow proof. Reviewer catches spec drift. Craft Judge catches merely functional or poorly shaped code. Doc-writer catches documentation lies. Reality-gate catches end-to-end theatre.
62: 
63: Reviewer, Craft Judge, and Reality Gate must stay distinct:
64: 
65: - Reviewer asks: Does this satisfy the spec?
66: - Craft Judge asks: Is this the best shape this code could reasonably have?
67: - Reality Gate asks: Is this proven live?
68: 
69: ## Role-Switching
70: 
71: Role-switching means one agent deliberately adopts a specialist lens for each phase. It is useful when:
72: 
73: - the work is tightly coupled;
74: - context continuity matters more than parallel speed;
75: - no paid API key is configured;
76: - the user wants cost-free rigour.
77: 
78: The method is:
79: 
80: 1. Read the relevant persona or role instructions.
81: 2. Do that phase's work only.
82: 3. Emit the phase output and verdict.
83: 4. Switch role deliberately before the next phase.
84: 
85: Role-switching is not pretending to be multiple minds. It is structured attention.
86: 
87: ## Sub-Agent Delegation
88: 
89: Sub-agents are useful when work can be split by independent question or disjoint write surface.
90: 
91: Good delegation:
92: 
93: - "Inspect source truth for these three modules and report current state."
94: - "Implement only the docs index file; another worker owns source tests."
95: - "Review the generated pack envelope against Prompt Guidelines."
96: 
97: Bad delegation:
98: 
99: - two agents editing the same file;
100: - a worker writing code that depends on a type another worker has not finished;
101: - delegating the immediate blocking task while the main agent waits;
102: - sending vague "look at this repo" tasks.
103: 
104: Parallelism is by file ownership and dependency independence, not by item number.
105: 
106: ## API Orchestrator
107: 
108: The Python orchestrator pattern drives roles through A-F, C2 where configured, or single-agent calls. It supports:
109: 
110: - full pack runs;
111: - selected phases;
112: - single-agent prompts;
113: - dry runs;
114: - agent listing;
115: - per-phase cost reports.
116: 
117: The cost tracker reports token use and cost by phase. That cost transparency is part of the method: paid agent work is a design decision, not an invisible default.
118: 
119: ## Codex Delegation Wrapper
120: 
121: The Codex delegation wrapper is a controlled handoff from one AI lane to Codex for code-writing tasks.
122: 
123: Its method:
124: 
125: 1. Caller authors a precise prompt.
126: 2. Wrapper runs `codex exec` in a controlled sandbox.
127: 3. Wrapper captures prompt, events, final message, diff, metadata, stderr, and git diff artefacts.
128: 4. Caller reviews the patch.
129: 5. Caller accepts, rejects, or partially accepts.
130: 
131: The wrapper does not decide. It executes and records.
132: 
133: Important invariants:
134: 
135: - clean target worktree at start;
136: - git repo required;
137: - absolute paths required;
138: - no unsandboxed danger mode;
139: - rate limit;
140: - wall-clock timeout;
141: - untracked files included in diff review;
142: - no automatic commit.
143: 
144: This makes delegated code reviewable before it becomes repository state.
145: 
146: ## Two-Lane Collaboration Pattern
147: 
148: Use complementary lanes when work needs both high-level craft pressure and repo-grounded implementation discipline. One lane pressure-tests architecture and craft; another grounds implementation in source evidence and proof.
149: 
150: The lanes are peers. Neither should be treated as downstream of the other.
151: 
152: Recommended high-quality workflow:
153: 
154: ```text
155: User intent
156: -> architecture/craft challenge
157: -> repo-grounded recon
158: -> blueprint or pack repair
159: -> implementation/proof
160: -> Reviewer/Tester pass
161: -> craft review
162: -> Reality Gate
163: -> handoff
164: ```
165: 
166: Use the architecture/review lane especially for:
167: 
168: - architecture pressure;
169: - concept shape;
170: - trade-offs;
171: - simplification attacks;
172: - craft review;
173: - detecting hidden compromises;
174: - judging whether the solution is elegant enough.
175: 
176: Use the implementation/proof lane especially for:
177: 
178: - repo-grounded reading;
179: - implementation;
180: - preserving file boundaries;
181: - proving behaviour;
182: - catching facade paths;
183: - producing auditable deltas;
184: - maintaining source-truth discipline.
185: 
186: The point is not hierarchy. It is complementary pressure: one lane challenges shape, the other grounds the work in source and proof.
187: 
188: ## Host Bridge
189: 
190: The host bridge exists when the AI environment cannot directly run the real machine's toolchain.
191: 
192: The bridge is file-based:
193: 
194: - agent writes a shell payload;
195: - payload declares protocol;
196: - trigger file or double-click starts execution;
197: - output is captured in a log;
198: - done flag records timestamp, exit code, and protocol.
199: 
200: The pattern supports:
201: 
202: - one-shot commands;
203: - persistent shell sessions for iterative loops;
204: - emergency recovery channel;
205: - audit logs;
206: - protocol headers;
207: - watcher mode for headless execution.
208: 
209: This keeps host-native proof separate from sandbox simulation.
210: 
211: ## Autopilot
212: 
213: Autopilot is the unattended version of the same method. It wakes on a schedule, picks work from a task queue, advances one bounded task under a time budget, logs what happened, and stops.
214: 
215: The method requires:
216: 
217: - halt files for pausing;
218: - wake locks to prevent concurrent overlap;
219: - stale-lock thresholds;
220: - bounded work budget;
221: - shared host-bridge lock when lanes share Terminal payloads;
222: - append-only logs;
223: - task status fields: pending, in-progress, done, blocked;
224: - clear "done when" criteria;
225: - no destructive delete in protected shared workspaces.
226: 
227: Autopilot is not an excuse to bypass proof. It still works under `[FULL]` or stop.
228: 
229: ## Dual-Lane Autopilot
230: 
231: A mature setup may use two unattended lanes:
232: 
233: | Lane | Queue | Log | Notes |
234: |---|---|---|---|
235: | Review/delegation lane | `tasks.md` | `log.md` | Delegates production code changes through a controlled wrapper. |
236: | Implementation/proof lane | `tasks.implementation.md` | `log.implementation.md` | Edits directly and writes proof reports. |
237: 
238: The lanes have separate queues and wake locks but share one git worktree and host bridge. They coordinate by not starting production edits while the other lane has a recent wake lock.
239: 
240: ## No Destructive Delete Pattern
241: 
242: In protected shared workspaces, agents avoid direct destructive deletes because host approval dialogs or permission gates can block unattended work.
243: 
244: Use the `PLEASE DELETE` pattern:
245: 
246: 1. Ensure `<repo-root>/PLEASE DELETE/` exists.
247: 2. Move the target there.
248: 3. Add an ISO timestamp suffix on collision.
249: 4. Keep `PLEASE DELETE/` gitignored.
250: 5. The user empties it manually.
251: 
252: This is an operational reliability method, not just a safety rule.
253: 
254: ## Cost Discipline
255: 
256: Every strategy that spends money must be explicit:
257: 
258: - name the mode;
259: - explain why cheaper modes are insufficient;
260: - estimate cost in the user's local currency;
261: - wait for approval;
262: - report actual cost after the run.
263: 
264: Free sub-agents and role-switching do not require cost approval, but they still require scope discipline.
265: 
266: ## Orchestration Decision Tree
267: 
268: 1. Is this a small, self-contained task? Use Solo.
269: 2. Does the task need multiple lenses but one context should hold everything? Use Role-Switch.
270: 3. Are there independent read-only questions or disjoint write sets? Use Sub-Agent Delegation.
271: 4. Does speed or external parallelism justify paid calls? Propose API Agent Team with a cost estimate.
272: 5. Is the task unattended and queue-driven? Use Autopilot if installed.
273: 6. Does the environment lack the real toolchain? Use Host Bridge for proof.
274: 7. Does another AI lane write code? Capture and review the diff before accepting.
275: 
276: ## Parallel-Agent Orchestration Patterns
277: 
278: When multiple AI colleagues work the same repo concurrently across different machines, coordinated by a human relay or by an orchestrator agent, the failure modes are not the same as solo work. The following patterns prevent the predictable collisions and stop-and-recover round trips that consume session time disproportionately.
279: 
280: ### Pre-Flight Checks On Each Dispatch Machine
281: 
282: Before assigning task work to a colleague on a remote machine, verify the machine is ready. Run, or have the dispatched colleague run as their first action, a four-line check:
283: 
284: ```text
285: git -C <repo> fetch
286: git -C <repo> status -uno
287: ssh-add -l
288: git -C <repo> push --dry-run 2>&1 | head -3
289: ```
290: 
291: What each catches:
292: 
293: - `git fetch` + `status -uno` — tree-staleness. Detects when a clone is many commits behind origin before any orientation work is done against stale state.
294: - `ssh-add -l` — credential availability. Detects empty SSH agent before mid-task push attempts fail.
295: - `push --dry-run` — credential gating end to end. Surfaces HTTPS keychain locks or missing tokens before the colleague is committed to work that cannot land.
296: 
297: If any check fails, fix credentials and sync state before dispatching real work. The cost of this pre-flight is seconds; the cost of skipping it is hours of stop-and-recover round trips.
298: 
299: ### Pre-Assigned Identity Slots
300: 
301: When two or more colleagues will both append themselves to a project's append-only contributor ledger (`agents/.identity.json`) in parallel sessions, both will see the same "next free slot" number and both will claim it. The merge between their commits will produce a collision on that file.
302: 
303: Prevention: the orchestrator (human or agent) pre-assigns identity slot numbers at dispatch time:
304: 
305: ```text
306: You are contributor #N. The parallel colleague(s) on the other machine(s) are
307: #N+1 [and #N+2]. Use exactly your assigned number when appending to
308: agents/.identity.json. Do not infer the next-free slot at runtime.
309: ```
310: 
311: This converts a predictable collision into a mechanical decision the orchestrator makes once. The cost is one extra line in each dispatch prompt.
312: 
313: Under the lineage archive convention (`Lineage/LINEAGE_ARCHIVE_CONVENTION.md`), the contributor ledger and profiles live in the archive repo at `<archive>/<repo-name>/agents/` rather than the focus repo. The same collision, observer-assignment, and merge-recovery logic in this section applies unchanged — it just runs against the archive repo's git history, and any `git show <commit>:agents/...` recovery is executed there.
314: 
315: ### Pre-Assigned Observer Roles
316: 
317: When two or more colleagues both arrive after the same predecessor in the lineage, both will read the standing instruction "complete the predecessor's observed sections" and both will fill the same fields in the predecessor's profile (`agents/profiles/<predecessor-profile-slug>.json`). The merge will drop one set, losing real observer work.
318: 
319: Prevention: assign the observer role to exactly one colleague per predecessor at dispatch:
320: 
321: ```text
322: You observe <predecessor-name>. The other parallel colleague(s) do not.
323: Complete only the predecessor you are assigned. If you encounter another
324: predecessor whose observed sections are also empty, leave them — a
325: different successor will fill them.
326: ```
327: 
328: The asymmetric-observer convention is in `Method/06_Ledgers_Memory_and_Handoffs.md`. Pre-assignment makes it operational under parallel orchestration.
329: 
330: ### Misroute-Prevention Preamble
331: 
332: In a relay-via-human dispatch model, the human pastes the orchestrator's prompt into each colleague's session. Misroutes are possible — paste-to-the-wrong-Mac, copy-paste-from-the-wrong-chat-window. Without an in-band check, the wrong colleague may start work on someone else's task.
333: 
334: Every dispatched prompt should open with an explicit name-verification preamble:
335: 
336: ```text
337: <working-name> — this message is for you specifically. You are the
338: colleague using the name <working-name>, working on <machine/role>.
339: If your name is not <working-name>, please stop and tell the relay
340: this prompt was misrouted; do not proceed.
341: ```
342: 
343: This converts misroute from a silent failure (the wrong colleague does the wrong work) into a loud one (the wrong colleague bounces it back). The cost is two lines per prompt.
344: 
345: ### Relay-Via-Human Trust-And-Paste Model
346: 
347: A common orchestration topology: one human relays messages between an orchestrator agent and one or more dispatched colleagues. The human does not read the relayed content in detail; they copy-paste it verbatim in both directions.
348: 
349: This asymmetric-attention model has two practical consequences:
350: 
351: 1. **Anything the human needs to read directly must be visually distinct.** Use bright emoji-block delimiters around action items and warnings addressed to the human; everything else in the message flows through opaquely:
352: 
353:    ```text
354:    🟠🟡🔵 ASH — please push commit <SHA> via GitHub Desktop. 🔵🟡🟠
355:    ```
356: 
357: 2. **Warnings, gating conditions, and action items must appear ABOVE any code block the human will paste.** The human scans top-to-bottom; once they hit a code block, they paste. A warning below the code block is read after the paste has already happened, which is too late. Order every response so the human-facing content comes first, then the relay payload.
358: 
359: Pre-assigned visual routing markers per colleague help the human relay without reading:
360: 
361: ```text
362: 🟣⬅️🟣 PASTE THIS TO <name-on-purple-machine>: 🟣⬅️🟣
363: 🟢➡️🟢 PASTE THIS TO <name-on-green-machine>: 🟢➡️🟢
364: 🔵⬆️🔵 PASTE THIS TO <name-on-blue-machine>: 🔵⬆️🔵
365: ```
366: 
367: The colors and arrow shapes are arbitrary; the discipline is using one distinct, stable marker per colleague so the human can route at a glance.
368: 
369: Symmetry note: the reverse direction needs the same convention. Anything a colleague wants the human to read directly (rather than relay to the orchestrator) must be wrapped in the same kind of emoji block. Otherwise it will be silently relayed without the human seeing it.
370: 
371: ### Sign-Off Convention For Multi-Colleague Sessions
372: 
373: When multiple colleagues are reporting through one relay channel, the orchestrator and human must be able to identify who said what at a glance. Require every colleague to end every message with a sign-off line:
374: 
375: ```text
376: — <working-name>
377: ```
378: 
379: And every output file with a final-line attribution:
380: 
381: ```text
382: Signed: <working-name>
383: ```
384: 
385: Combined with the per-colleague visual routing markers above, this makes a multi-agent transcript greppable and visually self-routing without requiring the human to read message bodies.
386: 
387: ### Coordinated Push Sequencing
388: 
389: When multiple parallel colleagues will all commit and push to the same repository, the order of pushes matters for conflict-resolution complexity. Push the colleague with the fewest stacked commits first; let them land clean on `origin/main`; then push the next colleague's stack and resolve the rebase against the now-known remote state.
390: 
391: If the orchestrator can see in advance that two colleagues' write surfaces overlap (the same file appears in both planned diffs), apply pre-assigned slots and observer roles to reduce the overlap before either pushes. If overlap remains, walk the second pusher through the rebase conflict resolution explicitly rather than letting them improvise.
392: 
393: ### Recovery From Merge-Loss Of Lineage Entries
394: 
395: If a parallel-write merge collision drops a colleague's contributor entry from `.identity.json` (because the merge chose the other colleague's version of the file), the orchestrator must restore the entry post-merge rather than treat the lost entry as resolved. The colleague's work in commits remains real; their absence from the ledger creates an orphan profile that violates KANON §15.
396: 
397: Recovery procedure:
398: 
399: 1. Extract the lost colleague's entry from their original commit: `git show <commit>:agents/.identity.json | <extract entry>`.
400: 2. Append the entry to the current `.identity.json` at the next available slot number (the slot they originally claimed will already be taken).
401: 3. Update the entry's slot reference in the contribution text if the colleague self-referenced their slot number; add a brief parenthetical noting the post-merge restoration so future readers see the history.
402: 4. Commit and push from a machine with working push credentials.
403: 
404: Do not leave the lost entry unrecovered. Lineage discipline (KANON §15) is non-optional.
405: 
406: ## Anti-Patterns
407: 
408: Avoid:
409: 
410: - asking a build agent to design missing semantics;
411: - asking a build agent to accept lower quality because the session is running long;
412: - using paid orchestration without cost approval;
413: - parallel agents writing the same file;
414: - treating a generated diff as accepted without review;
415: - letting an autopilot wake run without locks or logs;
416: - using sandbox output as proof for host-only toolchains;
417: - deleting shared files directly;
418: - hiding blocked work behind "partial success."
419: - treating working code as excellent without a craft-quality pass.
420: 
421: ## Success Standard
422: 
423: Agent orchestration is working when:
424: 
425: - the right role does the right phase;
426: - every handoff is bounded and auditable;
427: - cost is visible;
428: - write ownership is clear;
429: - shared worktree state is protected;
430: - toolchain proof is real;
431: - craft quality is judged separately from spec fidelity and live proof;
432: - future agents can see what happened and why.

