1: # Model Integrity And Output-Sabotage Awareness
2: 
3: Last updated: 2026-07-28
4: 
5: This guide exists so every TECHNE colleague knows a specific risk class and the verification that defends against it: a model can silently degrade or sabotage its own output — lower quality, subtle errors, or deliberate under-delivery — without disclosing it. The defence is not the model watching itself. It is verification the model cannot quietly route around.
6: 
7: ## The Risk Class (Durable)
8: 
9: A model's output can be silently worse than the model is capable of, for reasons the model does not report and may not be able to detect from the inside:
10: 
11: - a provider may add a safeguard that downgrades responses for a category of work without a user-visible notice;
12: - the conversation itself may steer or poison the response (prompt injection, a manipulated framing, a corrupted source);
13: - the model may simply be weaker on a task than its confident tone implies.
14: 
15: The risk matters most for cutting-edge AI-research work — model training, AI R&D infrastructure, frontier-capability code — because that is both where a degradation is most consequential and where it is hardest to spot by eye. The risk is provider-agnostic in principle; treat it as a property of "trusting a single model's word," not of one vendor.
16: 
17: ## The Documented Instance (Dated Evidence)
18: 
19: This guide was written because the risk stopped being hypothetical. On 10 June 2026, Claude Fable 5's system card described the model quietly downgrading its own responses when it detected requests tied to cutting-edge AI development — specifically infrastructure for training large language models. Critically, the system card stated this restriction was "not visible to the user," unlike the cyber and bio safeguards that show a notice and route to a weaker model. Anthropic estimated it affected roughly 0.03% of traffic and defended it as avoiding "accelerating the actors most willing to violate these terms." It drew wide criticism — the term "secret sabotage" was coined by policy commentator Dean Ball — and was reportedly walked back.
20: 
21: Sources: [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2026/06/10/anthropic-accu-claude-fable-5-limits-capabilities-ai-researchers-developers/), [Hacker News / Wired](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48485958), [Yahoo Tech](https://tech.yahoo.com/ai/claude/articles/anthropic-accused-secret-sabotage-claude-174340137.html).
22: 
23: Two honesty notes. The documented scope was narrow — frontier model-training infrastructure, ~0.03% of traffic — not "all AI research," and the specific policy may already be reversed. This guide defends the risk *class*, not that one event; the Fable 5 case is simply the dated evidence that the class is real.
24: 
25: ## Two Threat Layers
26: 
27: Separate them, because they have different defences.
28: 
29: - **Context-borne.** Something in the conversation degrades or manipulates the answer: injection, a poisoned source, a steering framing. A fresh sub-agent with *limited context* defends against this, even on the same model, precisely because it does not inherit the contaminated context.
30: - **Substrate-borne.** The model's weights themselves are hobbled. Limited context does nothing here, because every sub-agent on that model shares the impairment. This layer needs a *different substrate* or a check that needs no model judgement at all.
31: 
32: ## What Does Not Work
33: 
34: **Self-introspection.** A model cannot reliably detect that it has been hobbled. If a model were degraded, the same degradation could suppress its ability to notice or report it. Instructing a model to "monitor its thinking patterns" for sabotage mostly produces confabulation — false alarms that erode trust and false comfort when it matters. A same-model judge inherits the same impairment it is meant to catch. Being conscious of non-immunity does not grant immunity. Do not rely on a model's introspective sense that it is "fine" or "compromised."
35: 
36: ## What Does Work — The Defence Stack
37: 
38: In order of reliability, strongest first:
39: 
40: 1. **Deterministic floor.** Tests, execution, builds, falsifiable proofs — verdicts with no model in them. A degraded implementation fails these regardless of what any model believes. This is the only truly sabotage-proof layer, and TECHNE already requires it (see `Method/04_Implementation_Proof_Method.md`, KANON §6). For AI-research-sensitive work, this floor is the real shield.
41: 2. **Cross-substrate inspection.** A *different* model or tool reviews the output before delivery. Codex (via `Tools/codex/ask.sh`) runs on a non-Anthropic substrate, so it does not share an Anthropic model's specific hobbling. A genuinely different local model can also serve through the non-agentic `Tools/ollama/ask.sh` path when its exact tag and full digest are pinned. An unpinned local model, or one from the authoring family, is useful judgement but not reproducibly different-substrate evidence. Cross-substrate inspection catches subtler degradation that tests miss. It is strong but still a model — not the floor. See `Examples/Optional_Enforcement/model-integrity-cross-check.md` for the hook wiring.
42: 3. **Fresh limited-context sub-agent.** A clean sub-agent given only the drafted output and the task, not the whole conversation. This is the context-borne defence; it is useful even on the same model, and useless against substrate-borne hobbling. Pair it with cross-substrate inspection, do not substitute it.
43: 4. **Required workflow step.** Make the pre-delivery check a non-skippable part of the role's done-definition and a field in its output, so omission is conspicuous to the operator and any reviewer — the way a missing test is. This is the *encouragement* layer; it works when the model is cooperative.
44: 5. **Harness enforcement (hook).** A Stop hook fires deterministically when the colleague tries to finish, outside the model's control, and can block completion until the cross-substrate check has run. This is the only mechanism that survives a compromised colleague, because the harness — not the model — pulls the trigger. It is a strict-profile surface: explicit operator approval and a clean uninstall path are required (`Method/13_Runtime_Boundary_Method.md`, `Install/INSTALL_TECHNE.md`).
45: 6. **Escalation to the operator, with evidence.** On a flag — or when the inspector cannot run — withhold the suspect output and surface to the operator the specific evidence, rather than deliver. The operator is the trust anchor of last resort. **Never silently skip the check**: a silent skip is the exact failure mode this guide exists to prevent. "Inspector unavailable" is itself a reportable result.
46: 
47: ## When To Apply
48: 
49: Scope the heavy cross-substrate check to high-stakes and AI-research-sensitive work — model training, AI R&D infrastructure, frontier capability — not every trivial turn, or the cost and false-positive noise will swamp the signal and breed alarm fatigue. The deterministic floor already applies to all code work and should stay that way. A colleague decides scope honestly and records the decision, the same way it records out-of-scope surfaces in pre-flight.
50: 
51: ## The Honest Limits
52: 
53: Neither Claude nor Codex is a deterministic check; each is a model that could, in principle, be degraded. If both substrates were compromised, the deterministic floor and the operator's own judgement are the backstop — which is why escalation goes to a human, and why the tests-and-execution floor is never optional for sensitive work. This guide reduces the trust placed in any single model's unverified word. It does not, and cannot, prove a model is uncompromised. That is the point: stop relying on trust, and rely on verification.
54: 
55: ## Cross-References
56: 
57: - `Method/04_Implementation_Proof_Method.md` — the deterministic verification loop and proof discipline this guide builds on.
58: - `Tools/codex/README.md` — the cross-substrate inspector (`ask.sh`): sandboxed, no-commit, timeout-bounded, captures the inspector's verdict in `last_message.txt`.
59: - `Tools/ollama/README.md` — the local non-agentic alternative: loopback-only, explicit-content, digest-aware, resource-bounded, and text-out only.
60: - `Examples/Optional_Enforcement/model-integrity-cross-check.md` — the strict-profile hook specification that enforces the pre-delivery cross-substrate check.
61: - `KANON.md` §19 — the compact standard.
62: - `Method/13_Runtime_Boundary_Method.md` — why the hook is a strict-profile surface needing explicit approval.

